or:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;80,692&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Net Regulatory Liabilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:3pt double #000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:3pt double #000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;(42,190)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:3pt double #000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:3pt double #000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:3pt double #000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;(40,727)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:3pt double #000;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;These amounts are either included in the Respondent&#8217;s current rate filing, expected to be included in future rate proceedings, or covered by specific rate mechanisms, which govern the timing and amount of refunds or recovery. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Property, plant and equipment are recorded at cost. Depreciation is provided primarily on the straight-line, composite and group methods over estimated useful lives, generally 40 to 50 years on new property, pursuant to rates authorized by the FERC. Gains or losses from the ordinary sale or retirement of property, plant and equipment generally are credited or charged to accumulated depreciation; other gains or losses are recorded in net income. Depreciation was $64.5 million and $61.5 million for the years ended December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Gas Receivables/Payables&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In the course of providing transportation and storage services to customers, the Respondent may receive different quantities of natural gas from a shipper than quantities delivered on behalf of that shipper. These transactions result in imbalances, which are repaid or recovered in cash or through the receipt or delivery of natural gas in the future. Customer imbalances to be repaid or recovered in-kind are recorded in Miscellaneous Current and Accrued Liabilities (Account 242) or Miscellaneous Current and Accrued Assets (Account 174) on the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheets. Settlement of imbalances requires agreement between the pipeline and shippers as to allocations of volumes to specific transportation contracts and timing of delivery of natural gas based on operational conditions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent also uses gas from its system for compressor fuel and incurs gas losses during its normal course of operations. This gas is repaid in-kind from customers via a fuel reimbursement charge placed on the volume of gas transported through the system. Volumes due to or from the system as a result of fuel use or gas loss are also included in Miscellaneous Current and Accrued Liabilities (Account 242) or Miscellaneous Current and Accrued Assets (Account 174) on the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheets.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Natural gas receivables/payables are valued using a current published natural gas index price.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Cash Equivalents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent includes in cash equivalents any short-term highly-liquid investments that have an original maturity of three months or less when acquired.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In accordance with the Asset Retirement and Environmental Obligations Topic 410 of the ASC, the Respondent recorded an asset retirement obligation (ARO) for the remediation of asbestos existing on its system. The asbestos existing on the Respondent&#8217;s system is primarily in building materials and pipe coatings used prior to the promulgation in the 1970s of federal regulations prohibiting such uses of asbestos. The amount of the regulatory asset on the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheets was less than $0.1 million at December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024 where the related ARO liability was approximately $2.9 million and $2.8 million for the years ended December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, respectively. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Long-Lived Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Consistent with the Accounting for the Impairment or Disposal of Long-Lived Assets Topic of the ASC, the Respondent evaluates long-lived assets for impairment and assesses their recoverability based upon anticipated future cash flows. If facts and circumstances lead management to believe that the cost of an asset may be impaired, the Respondent will evaluate the extent to which that cost is recoverable by comparing the future undiscounted cash flows estimated to be associated with that asset to the asset&#8217;s carrying amount and reduce the carrying amount to fair market value to the extent necessary. During the years ended December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, the Respondent did not identify an impairment of its long-lived assets.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Derivative Instruments and Hedging Activities - Cash Flow Hedges&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent's objectives in using interest rate derivatives are to add stability to interest expense and to manage its exposure to interest rate movements. To accomplish these objectives, the Respondent primarily uses interest rate swaps and treasury lock interest rate agreements as part of its interest rate risk management strategy. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;For derivatives designated and that qualify as cash flow hedges of interest rate risk, the gain or loss on the derivative is recorded in Accumulated Other Comprehensive Income (OCI) (Account 219) and subsequently reclassified into Interest Expense (Account 427) in the same period or periods during which the hedged transaction affects earnings, as documented at hedge inception in accordance with the Respondent's accounting policy election. Amounts reported in accumulated OCI related to derivatives will be reclassified to interest expense as the transaction affects earnings on the Respondent's related debt.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Fair Value Measurements &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Fair Value Measurements and Disclosures Topic 820 of the ASC (ASC 820) which defines fair value, establishes a framework for measuring fair value in accordance with GAAP, and expands disclosures about fair value measurements to include the methods and assumptions used to measure fair value and the effect of fair value measures on earnings. ASC 820 requires the fair value of an asset or liability to be based on market-based measures which reflect the credit risk of the Respondent. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;3. REVENUE RECOGNITION&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent primarily earns revenues from contracts with customers by providing transportation and storage services for natural gas on a firm and interruptible basis. The Respondent had no contract assets or liabilities as of December&#160;31, 2025 or December 31, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;4. FINANCING &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;At December&#160;31, 2025 and December&#160;31, 2024, long-term debt consisted of the following: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:27pt"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:65.922%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:51.495%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:23.504%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.928%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:20.573%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1.01pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;December 31, 2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1.01pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;December 31, 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="9" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(In thousands)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Long-term debt:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 13.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;4.66% Notes due 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;100,000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;100,000&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 13.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;3.79% Notes due 2030&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;250,000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;250,000&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 13.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;5.68% Notes due 2034&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;125,000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;125,000&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Total long-term debt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;475,000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;475,000&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr style="height:3pt"&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:3pt double #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:3pt double #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Interest Rate Swap Agreement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On July 12, 2022, the Respondent entered into a pay-fixed receive-floating interest rate swap with a notional principal amount of $50.0 million set to mature on July 14, 2025. The interest rate swap is a cash flow hedge of the Respondent&#8217;s Term Loan that contains floating rate components. The objective of this agreement is to convert the variable rate component of the debt from a floating rate utilizing the one-month Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR), a benchmark interest rate, to a fixed rate stated in interest rate swap agreement. In conjunction with the Term Loan Retirement, the Respondent settled the related portion of the interest rate swap agreement and recognized a gain of approximately $0.4 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On September 21, 2022, the Respondent entered into a pay-fixed receive-floating interest rate swap with a notional principal amount of $50.0 million set to mature on July 14, 2025. The interest rate swap is a cash flow hedge of the Respondent&#8217;s Term Loan that contains floating rate components. The objective of this agreement is to convert the variable rate component of the debt from a floating rate utilizing the one-month SOFR, a benchmark interest rate, to a fixed rate stated in interest rate swap agreement. In conjunction with the Term Loan Retirement, the Respondent settled the related portion of the interest rate swap agreement and recognized a gain of approximately less than $0.1 million.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The fair value of the derivative instruments declared as a hedge under these strategies is recognized as either an asset or liability in the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheets. In conjunction with the Term Loan Retirement, the Respondent reclassed approximately $0.5 million of the derivatives&#8217; gains into Interest Expense on Long-Term Debt (Account 427). The Respondent recognized approximately $1.6 million through Interest Expense on Long-Term Debt (Account 427) as a gain on the swap agreements for the twelve months ended December 31, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Treasury Lock Interest Rate Agreements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In 2024, the Respondent entered into two treasury lock interest rate agreements. The treasury locks are cash flow hedges of forecasted transactions. The objective of the agreements is to effectively fix the treasury yield benchmark index of a forecasted transaction. The two treasury locks were terminated on October 23, 2024. The derivatives settled favorable for $3.4 million. It will decrease Interest Expense on Long-Term Debt (Account 427) by $3.4 million over the terms of the Respondent&#8217;s 5.68% Notes.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;On December 16, 2025, the Respondent entered into a treasury lock interest rate agreement that has a notional principal amount of $50.0 million. The treasury lock was terminated on March 17, 2026. The derivative settled favorably for less than $0.1 million, which will decrease interest expense by less than $0.1 million over the term of the newly signed/committed debt.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The fair value of derivative instruments declared as hedges under this strategy will be recognized as either an asset or liability in the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheets. The fair value of the new agreement was approximately less than $0.1 million as of December 31, 2025 and is recognized in Long-Term Portion of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Derivative Assets - Hedges (Account 176) on the accompanying Comparative Balance Sheets. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The related gains or losses on these contracts will be deferred in stockholders&#8217; equity as a component of Accumulated Other Comprehensive Income/Loss (Account 219) and subsequently reclassified into Interest Expense on Long-Term Debt (Account 427) as an adjustment to yield when the hedged transaction affects earnings. The Respondent has a policy to classify any proceeds/payments on the settlement of treasury locks as financing activities in the accompanying Comparative Statements of Cash Flows as the treasury locks are indirect financing elements.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:100.000%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:12.510%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:14.802%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:7.639%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:22.538%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:17.667%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:18.244%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="18" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Respondent's Notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Note Name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Face Value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Interest Rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Interest Payment Dates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Date Agreement Signed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Maturity Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;4.66% Notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:2.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;100,000,000&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:2.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;4.66&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;June 1 and December 1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;November 2, 2015&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;June 1, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;3.79% Notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;250,000,000&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;3.79&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;January 14 and July 14&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;July 14, 2020&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;July 14, 2030&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-left:0.02pt;padding-right:0.02pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;5.68% Notes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:6.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%;position:relative;top:-3.5pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;125,000,000&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;5.68&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;June 18 and December 18&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;November 6, 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;December 18, 2034&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-left:0.02pt;padding-right:0.02pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Term Loan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:6.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%;position:relative;top:-3.5pt;vertical-align:baseline"&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-right:-1.88pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;100,000,000&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Variable rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Monthly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;October 31, 2022&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1.02pt 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;July 14, 2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:3pt;padding-left:45pt;text-indent:-27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;padding-left:17.24pt"&gt;The issuance was delayed until December 18, 2024 and the proceeds were used to pay off the Respondent&#8217;s Term Loan.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:45pt;text-indent:-27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:8pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%;padding-left:17.24pt"&gt;On December 19, 2024, the Respondent retired its $100.0 million Term Loan.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Debt Covenants&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent&#8217;s 4.66% Notes, 3.79% Notes, and its 5.68% Notes each contain negative covenants that, subject to significant exceptions, limit the ability of the Respondent to, among other things, (i) incur debt, (ii) incur liens, (iii) engage in mergers, consolidations, liquidations and dissolutions, (iv) dispose of substantially all of the assets of the Respondent and its subsidiaries, (v) make certain restricted payments and (vi) enter into transactions with affiliates. The covenants require the Respondent to comply on a quarterly basis with a capitalization ratio. If an event of default occurs, the commitments of the Lenders to lend under the facility may be terminated and the maturity of the amounts outstanding may be accelerated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;5. COMMITMENTS AND CONTINGENCIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Regulatory and Rate Matters and Related Litigation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Fuel Filing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent recovers the natural gas it uses for fuel on its operating system and gas losses it incurs on its system in-kind from its customers via a fuel reimbursement charge placed on the volumes of gas transported through the system. The reimbursement charge is established through an annual fuel tracker filed with the FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:9pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental and Safety Matters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent is subject to federal, state and local statutes, rules and regulations relating to environmental protection, including the National Environmental Policy Act, the Clean Water Act, the Clean Air Act, the Toxic Substances Control Act, and the Resource Conservation and Recovery Act. Compliance with these laws and regulations can result in capital, operating and other costs, which generally subject the Respondent to inspections and require it to obtain and comply with a wide variety of environmental licenses, permits and other approvals. In addition, the Respondent is subject to requirements to report, investigate and clean up releases of pollutants found on or under properties that the Respondent owns or operates and any impacted neighboring properties. Many of the environmental programs are subject to frequent changes, based upon new legislation, new regulations, court challenges to regulations and changes in conditions affecting the Respondent&#8217;s operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent has identified polychlorinated biphenyl (PCB) contamination in air compressor systems, soils, and related properties at certain compressor station sites. All of the known areas of PCB contamination at compressor stations have been remediated except for two compressor station sites where PCB is encapsulated in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;concrete. The removal and remediation of PCBs at these two compressor stations will likely occur when the stations are decommissioned. The Respondent has accrued an undiscounted liability of approximately $0.6 million as of December&#160;31, 2025 and 2024, representing the current estimate of future environmental testing and cleanup costs (relating to known and suspected contamination by PCBs). The Respondent considers environmental assessment, remediation, and costs associated with compliance with environmental standards to be recoverable through rates, as they are prudent costs incurred in the ordinary course of business. The costs and timing of expenditures incurred will depend on the actual amount and extent of contamination discovered, the final cleanup standards mandated by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) or other governmental authorities, and the duration of related negotiations with governmental authorities, among other factors.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Greenhouse gases (GHG) including methane and carbon dioxide, are currently federally regulated air pollutants. New or modified sources of GHG may require obtaining operating permits and implementation of controls to reduce GHG emissions. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In June 2016, the EPA expanded its 2012 New Source Performance Standards for the oil and gas industry, NSPS OOOOa, to achieve both methane reductions and additional reductions in volatile organic compounds. The rule&#8217;s focus is specific to the oil and natural gas sectors, setting emission standards for methane and adding requirements to previously unaffected sources. The rule requires reductions in emissions from the Respondent&#8217;s operations and imposes pollution control and other requirements that affect the Respondent&#8217;s ability to build, replace or modify equipment. The standards have been under continuous judicial review. In November 2021, the EPA proposed changes to the existing rule OOOOa, and proposed two new subparts to the rule, one for new sources going forward, OOOOb, and one for existing sources, OOOOc. At this time, the final rule is being litigated throughout the Courts, and EPA has taken the regulation under reconsideration. Court decisions and/or EPA updates are expected in Q1/Q2 2026. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In February 2022, the EPA proposed a Federal Implementation Plan to control NOx from sources in 26 states in an effort to prevent non-attainment with the 2015 ozone standard in downwind states. The &#8220;Good Neighbor Rule&#8221; requires retrofits of engines over 1,000 hp in Oklahoma, Missouri and Wyoming. Until such changes are finalized, it is not possible to assess their impacts or predict the impacts on the Respondent&#8217;s business, financial position and operations. This rule has been stayed by the Supreme Court and is awaiting decision from the D.C. Circuit for a path forward. Their decision is expected sometime in 2026. This rule has also been addressed via reconsideration by the Trump Administration and appears to be permanently stayed at this time. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;In June 2022, the EPA published updates to the Mandatory Reporting Rule for GHG. Changes include higher emission factors for fugitive leaks and combustion emissions. This is currently under review by the EPA. The EPA is in the midst of significant deregulation and it is unclear at this time how this rule will be impacted. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;All of the Respondent&#8217;s facilities are located in areas currently designated as being in &#8220;attainment&#8221; of all National Ambient Air Quality Standards for all criteria air pollutants. These areas are subject to monitoring and periodic updates to their attainment classifications. Revised ambient air quality standards could result in certain areas being classified as non-attainment that were formerly in attainment status. If a non-attainment designation occurs in areas in which the Respondent operates, it could ultimately result in changes to the air pollution control requirements to which the Respondent is subject, and increase both its capital and operating costs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;Environmental laws and regulations are subject to frequent revisions through new legislation on the state or federal level, new regulations and court challenges and related decisions. The final results of any pending proposals and court cases, as well as future proposed legislative or regulatory changes, are generally difficult to predict until such changes are adopted in final legislation, regulations or court decisions. Until such changes are finalized, it is not possible to assess their impacts or predict the impacts on the Respondent&#8217;s business, financial position and operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;Summary of Commitments and Contingencies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;The Respondent is a party to various legal, regulatory and other matters arising from the day-to-day operations of our businesses that may result in claims against the Respondent. Although no assurance can be given, the Respondent believes, based on our experiences to date, that the ultimate resolution of such items will not have a material adverse impact to its business. The Respondent believes it has meritorious defenses to the matters to which it is a party and intends to vigorously defend itself. When the Respondent determines a loss is probable of occurring and is reasonably estimable, it accrues an undiscounted liability for such contingencies based on its best estimate using information available at that time. Because it presently is not possible to determine the outcome of these matters, no provision has been made in the financial statements for their ultimate resolution. In management&#8217;s opinion, the final resolution of all legal matters will not have a material adverse effect on the Respondent&#8217;s financial position. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:139%"&gt;6. INCOME TAXES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:6pt;margin-top:6pt;text-indent:27pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:139%"&gt;A summary of the income tax provision is as follows:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:27pt"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:64.434%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:53.634%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:20.378%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.954%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:20.378%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.956%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-left:0.02pt;padding-right:0.02pt;text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;For the Year Ended December 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000000;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:1.5pt;margin-top:3.75pt;padding-left:0.02pt;padding-right:0.02pt;text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;For the Year Ended December 31,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:700;line-height:100%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="12" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;(In thousands)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 1.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Current provision:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 13.02pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Federal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-alig